---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import * as stylex from "@stylexjs/stylex";
import { caseStudyStyles } from "../../styles/caseStudyStyles";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Apolloon Race Operations | Warre Snaet"
  description="How Apolloon runs a 24-hour relay without the cloud: Electron hosts that work offline, find each other on the LAN, and replicate their SQLite data."
  kicker="Paid client project · 2026"
  heading="Apolloon race operations"
  lead="I built the software for a 24-hour relay race. It runs on the event's wired LAN instead of a cloud service: every Electron host has its own Node backend and its own writable SQLite database."
  meta={[
    { label: "Role", value: "Software engineer · built it end to end" },
    { label: "Stack", value: "React, TypeScript, Express, tRPC, Socket.IO, SQLite, Electron" },
    { label: "Runtime", value: "Electron hosts · wired LAN · local SQLite" },
  ]}
>
  <CaseSection label="Purpose" title="Running a race without the cloud">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>For 24 hours, the software has to register runners, manage the queue, record laps, fix mistakes, show the live race state, and export results. None of that should stop because the Wi-Fi drops, the internet goes down, or a laptop dies.</p>
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>So Apolloon is deployed to the event LAN itself. One Electron laptop can run the whole event on its own. When several host laptops share a wired switch, each one keeps a full writable copy of the database and catches up after it loses its connection.</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list, caseStudyStyles.factsList)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Works alone</strong>One packaged host can run an event with no internet, no PostgreSQL, and no other managed service.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Hosts and thin clients</strong>Operator laptops and display screens that only run a browser connect over the LAN and don't store any data.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Live updates</strong>After a local write succeeds, connected screens get a small update over Socket.IO.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Runtime" title="A desktop app with its own backend">
    <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.architecture)}>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowRow)}>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>LAN clients</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Operator and display browsers</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Connect to an Electron host for race control, live state, imports, and exports</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Host runtime</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Electron + local Node process</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Bundles the React UI, the Express and tRPC API, the Socket.IO bridge, and static files</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Host storage</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>SQLite state and operation log</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Every host has its own writable data, and each local change also writes a record for replication</span></div>
      </div>
      <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowArrow)}>↓ signed UDP discovery + authenticated HTTP delta sync ↓</p>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowRow)}>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Pair once</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Persisted host identities</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>A new host joins with a creator URL and a short pairing code, and keeps a recovery copy before its data is replaced</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Rediscover routes</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>HMAC-signed UDP announcements</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Hosts find each other again after a reconnect or a LAN IP change, with no central server involved</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Reconcile state</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Missing operation deltas only</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Peers authenticate, check that they are compatible, and replay operations in a fixed canonical order</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Consistency" title="What a single write goes through">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>Before changing anything, the local server checks the command and whether the race is in a state that allows it. The data change and its immutable replication record are committed in one SQLite transaction. Only after that does the UI get a response and do connected clients get the live update.</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Exactly-once retries</strong>Sending a command ID again with the same payload returns the original result. Using that ID for a different write is rejected.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Atomic replication</strong>If an incoming batch is invalid, nothing changes: not the race data, not the operation log, and not the recorded progress for that peer.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Deterministic recovery</strong>When hosts that wrote at the same time reconnect, their histories can be rebuilt from a checkpoint in canonical operation order.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Durable local storage</strong>Every host runs SQLite in WAL mode with full synchronization in production.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Safety" title="No automatic failover, on purpose">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>Registration, the queue, and admin stay writable on every host. Timing works differently. One host is the timing controller, and a planned handover can only go to a peer that is reachable and fully caught up. When two laptops lose contact, they have no safe way to elect a new controller by themselves, so Apolloon doesn't try.</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list, caseStudyStyles.factsList)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Guarded timing takeover</strong>If the controller is lost, a caught-up replica can take over once an operator confirms it. When the system can't be sure it has every timing action, it shows a warning instead of hiding the gap.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Replication is not backup</strong>Every production host also takes a SQLite snapshot every five minutes, checks its integrity, and records it in a SHA-256 manifest.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Version checks</strong>Before exchanging any operations, peers compare their schema, replication format, app version, and release identity.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Cross-platform builds</strong>The same self-contained Electron app is packaged for Linux, Windows, and macOS.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Ownership" title="What I built">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>This is paid client work, so the code isn't public. This is the product and systems work I was responsible for:</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>The local-first architecture, the Electron runtime, the typed boundary between browser and server, the SQLite data model, and replication through the operation log.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Host discovery on the LAN, authenticated exchange of missing operations, compatibility checks between peers, reconnect handling, backups, and the safeguards around timing handover.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>The operator screens for registration, the queue, timing, corrections, imports, exports, and the live race state.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Regression and end-to-end tests for standalone writes, several hosts converging, catching up after a reconnect, rolling back invalid batches, peers changing address, and timing conflicts.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
